**Because React's default is to re-render the whole subtree below a component whose state changed; it does not compare props unless you opt in with `React.memo` (or the React Compiler does it for you). A re-render is just calling the function again, and it is usually cheap.**

**Why React works this way.** When state updates, React calls that component again to get a new element tree. The child elements in that tree are brand-new objects, so React has no cheap guarantee that the child's output is unchanged: a child might read context, a ref, or a module variable. Re-rendering everything is the safe default; reconciliation then diffs the output and only touches the DOM where something actually changed.

**Re-render is not a DOM update.**
- Render phase: call components, build elements, diff. Pure JS.
- Commit phase: apply only the differences to the DOM.

So a child re-rendering with identical output costs CPU time but no DOM writes.

**How to skip it when it matters:**

```tsx
const Row = memo(function Row({ item, onSelect }: Props) {
  return <li onClick={() => onSelect(item.id)}>{item.name}</li>;
});

function List({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const onSelect = useCallback((id: string) => setSelected(id), []);
  return <ul>{items.map((i) => <Row key={i.id} item={i} onSelect={onSelect} />)}</ul>;
}
```

`memo` shallow-compares props, so you also need stable references for objects and callbacks, otherwise the comparison always fails.

**Cheaper alternatives first:**
- Move state down into the component that actually uses it.
- Lift content up: pass expensive children as `children`. Those elements are created by the grandparent, so they keep the same reference when the parent's state changes and React bails out.

**Trade-off:** memoising everything adds comparison cost and noise. In 2026 the React Compiler auto-memoises most of this, so manual `memo` is for measured hot spots.

**Keys tell React which item is which between renders. With index keys, inserting, deleting or reordering shifts the identity: React reuses the component and DOM node at position 2 for whatever item is now at position 2, so local state, focus and uncontrolled input values end up attached to the wrong row.**

**The mechanism.** During reconciliation React matches old and new children by key. Same key means "same component instance, update its props". With indexes, removing the first item makes every key point at the next item: React does not delete row 0, it updates rows 0..n-1 with shifted props and deletes the last one.

```tsx
// Bug: type in the first input, then delete the first todo.
// The text you typed stays in the first row, now showing a different todo.
{todos.map((t, i) => (
  <li key={i}>{t.title} <input /></li>
))}

// Fix: a stable id from the data
{todos.map((t) => (
  <li key={t.id}>{t.title} <input /></li>
))}
```

**What breaks in practice:**
- Uncontrolled inputs, checkboxes and `useState` inside rows keep the old item's values.
- Animations and focus jump to the wrong element.
- Extra work: every shifted row re-renders with new props instead of one insert/delete.

**When index keys are fine:** the list is static, never reordered or filtered, and items have no state. A rendered list of fixed nav links is fine.

**What not to do:** `key={Math.random()}` gives a new key every render, so React unmounts and remounts every row, losing state and focus and killing performance.

**Bonus point:** changing a key on purpose is the idiomatic way to reset a component, e.g. `<ProfileForm key={userId} />` gives a fresh form state when the user changes, with no effect needed.

**The render phase is React calling your components to compute the next UI and diffing it against the previous one; it is pure and can be paused, restarted or thrown away. The commit phase is when React applies the resulting changes to the DOM, attaches refs and runs effects; it is synchronous and cannot be interrupted.**

**Render phase:**
- Triggered by initial mount, a state update, or a parent re-render.
- React calls component functions, which return elements, and reconciles them with the current fiber tree to find what changed.
- With concurrent features (transitions, Suspense) React can yield to the browser mid-render, abandon a render if newer state arrives, and retry later. That is why render must be pure: same props and state in, same output, no side effects.

**Commit phase:**
1. Mutate the DOM (insertions, updates, deletions).
2. Set refs, run `useLayoutEffect` callbacks synchronously, before the browser paints.
3. Browser paints.
4. Run `useEffect` callbacks after paint (passive effects).

```tsx
function Price({ value }: { value: number }) {
  // render phase: pure computation only
  const label = formatCurrency(value);

  // commit phase work goes in effects
  useEffect(() => {
    analytics.track("price_seen", { value });
  }, [value]);

  return <span>{label}</span>;
}
```

**Why it matters in interviews and in prod:**
- A side effect in the render body might run twice (StrictMode) or for a render that is discarded, causing duplicate requests or logs.
- A "slow component" is usually slow in render (expensive computation, too many children), not in commit; the Profiler shows both separately.
- Reading layout (measure an element) belongs in `useLayoutEffect`, because the DOM exists only after commit.

**Because `count` is a constant snapshot from the render the handler was created in. Both calls compute `0 + 1` and queue "set to 1". To build on the latest queued value, use the updater form: `setCount(c => c + 1)`.**

**The mechanism.**
- Each render has its own props and state. `count` inside the handler is a plain `const` captured by the closure; calling `setCount` does not mutate it.
- `setCount(value)` does not re-render immediately. React queues the update and batches all updates from the same event (and since React 18, from promises, timeouts and native handlers too) into a single re-render.
- When React processes the queue, a value replaces state, while an updater function receives the result of the previous queued update.

```tsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  function addTwoWrong() {
    setCount(count + 1); // queue: replace with 1
    setCount(count + 1); // queue: replace with 1
    console.log(count);  // still 0
  }

  function addTwo() {
    setCount((c) => c + 1); // 0 -> 1
    setCount((c) => c + 1); // 1 -> 2
  }
  return <button onClick={addTwo}>{count}</button>;
}
```

**Rule of thumb:** if the next state depends on the previous state, use the updater. It also avoids stale-closure bugs in intervals and async callbacks.

**Related details:**
- Batching means one render for many updates, which is good for performance.
- `flushSync` forces a synchronous update, e.g. to scroll to a newly added item right away. It is an escape hatch and hurts performance if overused.
- With complex related state, `useReducer` makes transitions explicit and always receives the current state.

**A stale closure is a function that captured variables from an old render and keeps using those old values after state has changed. Fix it by declaring the right dependencies, using the updater form of setState, reading the latest value from a ref, or using `useEffectEvent` for non-reactive logic inside effects.**

**Why it happens.** Every render creates new functions that close over that render's props and state. If an effect, interval, subscription or memoised callback was created once and never recreated, it still sees the values from the render it was born in.

```tsx
function Ticker() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      setCount(count + 1); // stale: count is always 0, so it sticks at 1
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);
}
```

**Fixes, in order of preference:**
- **Updater function:** `setCount(c => c + 1)` needs no outer value at all.
- **Correct deps:** list what the effect reads and let it re-run. Fine when re-subscribing is cheap.
- **`useEffectEvent` (stable in React 19.2):** wrap logic that should see the latest props but should not re-trigger the effect, e.g. logging with the current theme on a socket message.
- **Ref for the latest value:** `latest.current = value` each render, read `latest.current` inside a long-lived callback. Good for third-party listeners.

```tsx
const onMessage = useEffectEvent((msg: Msg) => {
  notify(msg, theme); // always the current theme
});
useEffect(() => {
  const ws = connect(roomId);
  ws.on("message", onMessage);
  return () => ws.close();
}, [roomId]); // reconnects only when the room changes
```

**Real-world example:** a chat that reconnects whenever the theme toggles, because `theme` was in the deps only to read it when logging. `useEffectEvent` removes the reconnect.

Trust the exhaustive-deps lint rule; disabling it is how stale closures ship.

**Use `useState` for values that affect what is rendered: changing them must trigger a re-render. Use `useRef` for values you need to remember between renders that do not affect output: DOM nodes, timer ids, the previous value, an AbortController, or the latest callback.**

**The difference in mechanism:**
- `useState` returns a snapshot; the setter schedules a re-render with the new value.
- `useRef` returns the same mutable object `{ current }` on every render. Mutating `current` does not re-render anything, and the change is visible immediately.

```tsx
function Stopwatch() {
  const [elapsed, setElapsed] = useState(0);      // shown on screen
  const timerId = useRef<number | null>(null);    // bookkeeping only
  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null); // DOM node

  function start() {
    if (timerId.current) return;
    timerId.current = window.setInterval(() => setElapsed((e) => e + 1), 1000);
  }
  function stop() {
    if (timerId.current) clearInterval(timerId.current);
    timerId.current = null;
  }
  return <><input ref={inputRef} /><button onClick={start}>{elapsed}s</button></>;
}
```

**Common ref use cases:**
- Focusing, measuring or scrolling a DOM element.
- Holding an interval id, WebSocket or AbortController across renders.
- Storing the latest value for a long-lived callback to avoid stale closures.
- Tracking "has this mounted" or the previous prop for comparisons.

**Rules:** do not read or write `ref.current` during render (except lazy initialisation); do it in effects and event handlers. Rendering must be predictable from props and state, and refs are invisible to React.

**React 19 note:** `ref` is now a normal prop on function components, so `forwardRef` is no longer needed for new code, and callback refs can return a cleanup function.

**Context is for passing rarely-changing values down the tree without prop drilling: theme, locale, current user, a service instance. Pick a store like Zustand or Redux when shared client state changes often and many components need only slices of it, because stores let each component subscribe to a selector and re-render only when its slice changes.**

**Why the distinction matters.**
- Context has no selectors. Every component that calls `useContext(Ctx)` re-renders when the provider's value changes, even if it uses one field.
- Stores live outside React and use `useSyncExternalStore` under the hood; a component re-renders only when its selected value changes.

```tsx
// Zustand: components subscribe to slices
const useCart = create<CartState>()((set) => ({
  items: [],
  add: (item) => set((s) => ({ items: [...s.items, item] })),
}));

function CartBadge() {
  const count = useCart((s) => s.items.length); // re-renders only on count change
  return <span>{count}</span>;
}
```

**Decision guide:**
- **Local state** first; lift only as far as needed.
- **Context:** low-frequency values, or to inject a store instance per subtree (e.g. per-request store in SSR).
- **Zustand / Jotai:** small API, selectors, medium apps, editor-like UIs with lots of shared client state.
- **Redux Toolkit:** big teams that want strict conventions, middleware, devtools time travel and an event log of actions.
- **Server data:** none of the above. Use TanStack Query, SWR, or Server Components; they handle caching, staleness and refetching.
- **URL state:** filters, tabs and pagination belong in search params so they are shareable.

**Trade-off:** a store is another dependency and global mutable state; context is built in. Many apps need only context plus a server cache.

**Client UI state is owned by the browser and is always correct (is the modal open, what is typed in the input). Server data is a cached snapshot of something owned elsewhere: it can be stale the moment it arrives, other users change it, and it needs fetching, caching, deduping, refetching and invalidation. Treat it as a cache, not as state you own.**

**Properties of server state:**
- Asynchronous, with loading and error states.
- Shared: many components want the same resource.
- Stale over time; needs background refresh and invalidation after mutations.
- Expensive: you want deduplication and caching across navigations.

**Properties of UI state:**
- Synchronous and local: toggles, selections, form drafts, hover.
- Usually lives in `useState`, `useReducer`, a small store, or the URL.

```tsx
// Server state: a cache with keys and invalidation
const { data: todos, isPending } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ["todos", listId],
  queryFn: () => api.todos(listId),
  staleTime: 30_000,
});
const qc = useQueryClient();
const add = useMutation({
  mutationFn: api.addTodo,
  onSuccess: () => qc.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["todos", listId] }),
});

// UI state: local
const [filter, setFilter] = useState<"all" | "done">("all");
const shown = todos?.filter((t) => filter === "all" || t.done);
```

**The anti-pattern:** fetch in an effect, `setTodos(data)` into Redux, then hand-write refetch logic after every mutation. You end up re-implementing a cache badly, with duplicated sources of truth.

**In a Next.js App Router app** the server-data role shifts to Server Components plus `revalidateTag`/`updateTag`, and the client mostly holds UI state.

Derive, do not duplicate: compute filtered lists from the cached data instead of storing them.

**Optimistic UI shows the result of a mutation immediately, assuming the server will succeed, then reconciles with the real response. To roll back you keep a snapshot (or let React derive the optimistic state from the real state) and restore it on error, then tell the user.**

**React 19's built-in way: `useOptimistic`.** The optimistic value exists only while an action or transition is pending; when it settles, React falls back to the real state, so rollback is automatic if you do not commit the change.

```tsx
function Likes({ count, like }: { count: number; like: () => Promise<void> }) {
  const [optimistic, addOptimistic] = useOptimistic(count, (c, n: number) => c + n);

  async function onLike() {
    startTransition(async () => {
      addOptimistic(1);       // UI shows count + 1 instantly
      try { await like(); }   // server action; parent re-renders with real count
      catch { toast.error("Could not like"); } // pending ends: back to real count
    });
  }
  return <button onClick={onLike}>{optimistic}</button>;
}
```

**Classic way with a client cache (TanStack Query):**
- `onMutate`: cancel in-flight queries, snapshot the current cache, write the optimistic value, return the snapshot.
- `onError`: restore the snapshot.
- `onSettled`: invalidate to sync with the server truth.

**Design points:**
- Give optimistic items a temporary id and swap it for the server id on success.
- Concurrent updates: derive from the latest real state plus a queue of pending changes, not from a stale snapshot.
- Keep the server idempotent so retries do not double-apply.

**When not to:** payments, deletes that cannot be undone, anything where a false "success" is costly. Use a pending state instead.

Good fits: likes, toggles, reordering, chat messages, todo checkboxes.

**`React.memo` only skips re-renders caused by the parent when every prop is shallowly equal. It does not help when props are new references each render, when the component re-renders from its own state or a context it reads, when the render is cheap anyway, or when props genuinely change every time.**

**Cases where memo is useless:**
- **Unstable props:** `style={{ margin: 8 }}`, `onClick={() => ...}`, `items={data.filter(...)}`, or JSX `children` all create new references, so comparison always fails.
- **Context:** a consumer re-renders on context change regardless of memo.
- **Own state or store subscription:** memo is about props from the parent only.
- **Props always change:** a clock or a live value; you pay comparison cost for nothing.
- **Cheap components:** comparing props can cost as much as rendering a small leaf.

```tsx
const Chart = memo(function Chart({ data, options }: ChartProps) { /* heavy */ });

function Page({ raw }: { raw: Point[] }) {
  // memo defeated: both props are new every render
  return <Chart data={raw.map(scale)} options={{ grid: true }} />;
}

// fixed
const OPTIONS = { grid: true } as const;
function Page2({ raw }: { raw: Point[] }) {
  const data = useMemo(() => raw.map(scale), [raw]);
  return <Chart data={data} options={OPTIONS} />;
}
```

**Custom comparator** (`memo(C, areEqual)`) can work around this but is risky: if it ignores a prop that matters (like a callback that closes over new state) you get stale UI.

**Better structure often beats memo:** move state down, pass slow subtrees as `children`, or virtualise big lists.

**2026 note:** with the React Compiler enabled, components and their props are memoised automatically, so manual `memo` becomes rare. You still need to understand why reference identity matters, because the compiler bails out of components that break the rules of React.

**Do not put 50,000 rows in the DOM. Virtualise (window) the list so only the rows in view plus a small overscan are mounted, keep row components cheap and stable, and move filtering and sorting off the critical path. Better still, ask whether the user needs 50,000 rows at once, or pagination and server-side search.**

**Why:** the cost is DOM nodes, layout and memory, not React diffing. 50k rows times ~10 nodes is 500k nodes: slow initial render, janky scroll, huge memory.

**Virtualisation:** a scroll container with the full height, and only ~30 absolutely-positioned rows rendered for the current scroll offset.

```tsx
import { useVirtualizer } from "@tanstack/react-virtual";

function Rows({ rows }: { rows: Row[] }) {
  const parentRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const v = useVirtualizer({
    count: rows.length,
    getScrollElement: () => parentRef.current,
    estimateSize: () => 36,
    overscan: 10,
  });
  return (
    <div ref={parentRef} style={{ height: 600, overflow: "auto" }}>
      <div style={{ height: v.getTotalSize(), position: "relative" }}>
        {v.getVirtualItems().map((item) => (
          <RowView key={rows[item.index].id} row={rows[item.index]}
            style={{ position: "absolute", top: 0, transform: `translateY(${item.start}px)` }} />
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Supporting work:**
- Variable heights: measure rows (`measureElement`) and cache sizes.
- Filtering: `useDeferredValue(query)` so typing stays responsive; for heavy work use a Web Worker.
- Keep rows memoised with stable keys; avoid per-row context subscriptions.
- `content-visibility: auto` is a cheap CSS alternative for long but not huge lists.

**Trade-offs:** virtualisation breaks Ctrl+F, complicates accessibility (use proper grid roles, `aria-rowcount`/`aria-rowindex`, keyboard handling) and scroll restoration. For very dense data (spreadsheets, logs with millions of lines), canvas-based grids win.

**Product answer:** most users need server-side pagination or infinite scroll with search, not 50k rows in memory.

**The React Compiler (v1.0, stable since October 2025) is a build-time transform that automatically memoises components, JSX, computed values and callbacks based on what each piece depends on. In compiled code you rarely need `useMemo`, `useCallback` or `React.memo` for performance; they become escape hatches.**

**How it works:**
- It analyses each component and hook, assuming they follow the Rules of React (pure render, props and state treated as immutable, hooks called unconditionally).
- It emits code that caches intermediate values in a per-component slot array and reuses them when inputs are unchanged, at a finer grain than manual `useMemo` (even conditionally).
- Result: stable references for props passed to children, so children skip re-rendering, without `memo` wrappers.

```tsx
// You write
function Table({ rows, query }: Props) {
  const visible = rows.filter((r) => r.name.includes(query));
  const onPick = (id: string) => select(id);
  return <Grid rows={visible} onPick={onPick} />;
}
// The compiler caches `visible` on [rows, query] and `onPick` on [select],
// and the <Grid> element itself, so Grid only re-renders when they change.
```

**What it does not fix:**
- Code that breaks the rules (mutating props, reading `ref.current` in render, side effects in render). The compiler skips such components; the eslint plugin reports why.
- Expensive work that runs once per change anyway; memoisation only avoids repeats.
- Architecture: huge contexts, too much DOM, waterfalls.

**Rolling it out:**
- Enable `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` compiler rules first and fix violations.
- Turn it on incrementally (opt-in per directory or with `"use memo"`), and use `"use no memo"` to exclude a problem component.
- Next.js supports it via `reactCompiler: true`.

**Existing `useMemo`:** leave it; the compiler works alongside it. Keep manual memoisation where you need a guaranteed stable reference for an effect dependency or a third-party library.

**A controlled input gets its value from React state (`value` + `onChange`), so React is the single source of truth. An uncontrolled input keeps its own value in the DOM; you set an initial `defaultValue` and read it when needed via a ref or `FormData`.**

**Controlled:**
- Pros: instant validation, formatting and masking, conditional UI, easy reset, value is always in state.
- Cons: re-renders the component on every keystroke; heavy forms can feel slow if the whole form re-renders.

**Uncontrolled:**
- Pros: less code, no per-keystroke renders, works with native validation and progressive enhancement.
- Cons: harder to react to changes live; value is only read on submit or via refs.

```tsx
// Controlled
const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
<input value={email} onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)} />

// Uncontrolled with a React 19 form action
function Signup() {
  async function action(formData: FormData) {
    await createUser(String(formData.get("email")));
  }
  return (
    <form action={action}>
      <input name="email" type="email" defaultValue="" required />
      <button>Sign up</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

**Common bugs:**
- `value` without `onChange`: React makes the input read-only and warns.
- `value={user.name}` where `name` starts `undefined`: the input starts uncontrolled then becomes controlled. Use `value={user.name ?? ""}`.
- File inputs are always uncontrolled.

**How to choose:** use controlled when the UI must react to each keystroke (search-as-you-type, live formatting). Use uncontrolled for simple submit-and-validate forms, especially with React 19 actions. React Hook Form uses uncontrolled inputs with refs for performance and subscribes components to the fields they care about.

**Both run after React commits to the DOM. `useLayoutEffect` runs synchronously before the browser paints, so you can measure layout and make changes the user never sees in an intermediate state. `useEffect` runs after paint and does not block it, so it is the default for everything else.**

**Timing in a commit:**
1. React updates the DOM.
2. `useLayoutEffect` callbacks run (blocking).
3. Browser paints.
4. `useEffect` callbacks run.

**Use `useLayoutEffect` when you read layout and immediately write something that affects it:** tooltip or popover positioning, measuring text to truncate, scroll position restoration, syncing a third-party DOM library before paint.

```tsx
function Tooltip({ anchor, text }: { anchor: DOMRect; text: string }) {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const [top, setTop] = useState(0);

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const h = ref.current!.getBoundingClientRect().height;
    setTop(anchor.top - h - 8); // re-render happens before paint: no flicker
  }, [anchor, text]);

  return <div ref={ref} style={{ position: "fixed", top, left: anchor.left }}>{text}</div>;
}
```

With `useEffect`, the user could briefly see the tooltip at `top: 0` before it jumps.

**Use `useEffect` for:** subscriptions, analytics, timers, connecting to sockets, syncing with non-visual external systems.

**Costs and gotchas:**
- Layout effects block paint; heavy work there hurts responsiveness (INP).
- They do not run on the server. Code that must match SSR output should avoid relying on them for initial content.
- `useInsertionEffect` runs even earlier, for CSS-in-JS libraries to inject styles; app code rarely needs it.

**Often you need neither:** derive values during render, handle user events in handlers, and fetch on the server.

**A custom hook is a function starting with `use` that composes other hooks to package reusable stateful logic. A good one has one responsibility, a small typed API, handles its own cleanup, returns stable values, and hides the implementation (fetch, socket, storage) behind a domain name like `useOnlineStatus` rather than a generic `useEffectOnce`.**

**Key facts:**
- Hooks share logic, not state. Each component calling `useCart()` gets its own state unless the hook reads from context or an external store.
- If the function calls no hooks, it should not be a hook; make it a plain utility.
- It must follow the Rules of Hooks: called at the top level, same order every render.

**Example: a reusable, safe debounced value:**

```tsx
export function useDebouncedValue<T>(value: T, delayMs = 300): T {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delayMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value, delayMs]);
  return debounced;
}

// usage
const q = useDebouncedValue(search, 250);
const results = useQuery({ queryKey: ["search", q], queryFn: () => api.search(q) });
```

**Design checklist:**
- Name it after the purpose (`useWindowSize`, `useFeatureFlag`), not the mechanism.
- Accept options as an object once there are more than two parameters.
- Return a tuple for state-like pairs, an object when there are many named values.
- Keep returned callbacks stable (or rely on the React Compiler) so consumers can use them in deps.
- Always clean up subscriptions, timers and listeners.
- For external stores, use `useSyncExternalStore` rather than `useState` plus `useEffect`, so it is tear-free in concurrent rendering and works with SSR.

**Testing:** `renderHook` from Testing Library, with fake timers for time-based hooks, or test through a small component that uses it.

**Smell:** a hook that takes ten flags and does five things. Split it.

**Suspense declares a loading boundary: when a child suspends (waiting on data or code), React shows the nearest fallback. Transitions mark an update as non-urgent, so React keeps showing the current UI instead of a fallback and can interrupt the work for urgent input. `useDeferredValue` gives you a lagging copy of a value so expensive re-renders happen in the background while the input stays responsive.**

**Suspense:**
- A component suspends by reading something not ready yet: `use(promise)`, a lazy component, or a Suspense-enabled library (TanStack Query suspense mode, RSC streaming).
- Boundaries let you design the loading sequence: shell first, details later.

**`useTransition` / `startTransition`:**
- Urgent updates (typing, clicking) render immediately; transition updates render at lower priority and can be abandoned if newer input arrives.
- If a transition causes an already-visible boundary to suspend, React keeps the old UI and gives you `isPending` instead of flashing a spinner.
- React 19 allows async functions in transitions (Actions), with pending state tracked across the await.

```tsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const deferred = useDeferredValue(query);
  const isStale = query !== deferred;

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>
        <div style={{ opacity: isStale ? 0.6 : 1 }}>
          <Results query={deferred} /> {/* suspends or renders slowly */}
        </div>
      </Suspense>
    </>
  );
}

const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
const selectTab = (t: Tab) => startTransition(() => setTab(t));
```

**When to use which:**
- You own the state setter: `startTransition` (tab switch, navigation, filter apply).
- You only receive the value (a prop or input state): `useDeferredValue`.
- Never wrap the controlled input's own update in a transition; the input must update urgently.

**Versus debouncing:** deferral has no fixed delay; on a fast device the deferred render happens almost immediately, and it is interrupted rather than queued.

**Server Components run only on the server (at build or request time). They can be async, read databases and secrets directly, and their code never ships to the browser; React serialises their output (the RSC payload) and the client merges it into the tree. Client Components (`"use client"`) are the interactive islands that hydrate and can use state, effects and browser APIs.**

**Mental model: two module graphs joined by a boundary.**
- Everything is a Server Component by default (in frameworks like Next.js App Router).
- `"use client"` marks an entry point into the client graph; that file and everything it imports become client code.
- Server Components can render Client Components and pass them serialisable props: primitives, plain objects, arrays, Dates, Promises, JSX, and Server Functions. Not regular functions or class instances.
- Client Components cannot import Server Components, but can receive them as `children` or other JSX props.

```tsx
// app/products/page.tsx (Server Component)
export default async function Page() {
  const products = await db.product.findMany(); // runs on the server only
  return (
    <ul>
      {products.map((p) => (
        <li key={p.id}>
          {p.name} <AddToCart id={p.id} /> {/* client island */}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// add-to-cart.tsx
"use client";
export function AddToCart({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const [pending, start] = useTransition();
  return <button disabled={pending} onClick={() => start(() => addToCart(id))}>Add</button>;
}
```

**Why it matters:**
- Smaller bundles: markdown parsers, date libraries and data-access code stay on the server.
- No client-server waterfalls: data is fetched next to the component that needs it.
- Security: secrets stay server-side (use the `server-only` package to enforce it).

**Not SSR:** SSR turns components into HTML for the first load and still ships their JS to hydrate. RSC components never hydrate. They are complementary; Next.js does both.

**Costs:** a new boundary to reason about, serialisation limits, and a framework requirement.

**Use `useState` for independent, simple values. Pick `useReducer` when several pieces of state change together, when the next state depends on the previous one in non-trivial ways, or when you want the transitions named and testable as a pure function outside the component.**

**What `useReducer` gives you:**
- All transitions in one place: `(state, action) => newState`, easy to read and unit test.
- Impossible states are easier to avoid: a `status` field instead of separate `isLoading`, `isError`, `data` booleans that can contradict each other.
- A stable `dispatch` identity, safe to pass down or through context without memoising.

```tsx
type State =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "success"; data: User }
  | { status: "error"; error: string };

type Action =
  | { type: "fetch" }
  | { type: "resolve"; data: User }
  | { type: "reject"; error: string };

function reducer(_s: State, a: Action): State {
  switch (a.type) {
    case "fetch": return { status: "loading" };
    case "resolve": return { status: "success", data: a.data };
    case "reject": return { status: "error", error: a.error };
  }
}

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { status: "idle" });
```

**Rules:** reducers must be pure: no fetches, no randomness, no mutation; return new objects. Side effects go in event handlers or effects that dispatch results.

**When it is overkill:** a toggle, a single input, a counter. `useState` is clearer.

**Pattern:** `useReducer` plus context (state and dispatch in separate contexts) is a lightweight alternative to Redux for a medium-sized feature such as a multi-step checkout wizard.

**In React 19 you can pass a function as `<form action={fn}>`. React calls it with the `FormData` inside a transition, tracks pending state, and resets uncontrolled fields on success. `useActionState` wraps an action to give you its last returned state (errors, messages) plus a pending flag; `useFormStatus` lets a child, such as a submit button, read the form's pending status.**

**Why it is an improvement:** no `preventDefault`, no manual `isSubmitting`, built-in error and pending handling, and with Server Functions (Next.js Server Actions) the form even works before JavaScript loads (progressive enhancement).

```tsx
"use client";
import { useActionState } from "react";
import { useFormStatus } from "react-dom";
import { signup } from "./actions"; // a "use server" function

type FormState = { error?: string; ok?: boolean };

export function SignupForm() {
  const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState<FormState, FormData>(signup, {});
  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <input name="email" type="email" required />
      {state.error && <p role="alert">{state.error}</p>}
      <SubmitButton />
    </form>
  );
}

function SubmitButton() {
  const { pending } = useFormStatus(); // must be inside the <form>
  return <button disabled={pending}>{pending ? "Saving..." : "Sign up"}</button>;
}
```

```ts
// actions.ts
"use server";
export async function signup(_prev: FormState, fd: FormData): Promise<FormState> {
  const parsed = z.object({ email: z.email() }).safeParse({ email: fd.get("email") });
  if (!parsed.success) return { error: "Enter a valid email" };
  await createUser(parsed.data.email);
  return { ok: true };
}
```

**Details:**
- The action receives previous state first when used with `useActionState`.
- Return errors as data; throw only for unexpected failures (caught by an error boundary).
- Combine with `useOptimistic` for instant feedback.
- Always validate on the server; the action is a public endpoint.

**When not to:** complex client-heavy forms with live validation and dynamic field arrays may still be better with React Hook Form, which can also call an action on submit.

**In development only, StrictMode deliberately renders components twice and runs mount, cleanup, mount for effects, to surface impure renders and missing cleanup. Production builds do not do this. If double-invoking breaks your component, it has a bug that will show up later in real usage, for example with Fast Refresh, Activity/offscreen or remounts.**

**What it does:**
- Calls component bodies, initialisers and reducers twice and discards one result: catches side effects in render (mutating a shared array, logging, incrementing a global).
- Mounts, unmounts and remounts every component once: catches effects without cleanup (duplicate subscriptions, listeners, intervals, double connections).
- Also double-invokes ref callbacks in React 19, and warns about deprecated APIs.

```tsx
// Bug exposed by StrictMode: two listeners, never removed
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
}, []);

// Fixed: symmetric setup and cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
  return () => window.removeEventListener("resize", onResize);
}, []);
```

**The double fetch:** fetching in an effect fires twice in dev. The fix is not a `hasFetched` ref; it is an AbortController or ignore flag in cleanup, or better, a data library or server fetching that dedupes requests.

**Why React does this:** concurrent features can mount, hide and restore components while preserving state (the `<Activity>` component in React 19.2, Fast Refresh, back/forward cache). Effects must tolerate setup, cleanup, setup.

**Non-idempotent side effects** like "send analytics on mount" should be tolerant of duplicates or moved to event handlers where they belong.

**`createPortal(children, domNode)` renders children into a different DOM node, usually `document.body`, while keeping them in the same place in the React tree. You need it for modals, tooltips, toasts and dropdowns that must escape a parent's `overflow: hidden`, `transform` or stacking context (`z-index`).**

**Key behaviour:**
- **React tree, not DOM tree:** context, state and props work as if the portal content were rendered inline.
- **Events bubble through the React tree:** a click inside a portaled modal triggers `onClick` on React ancestors, even though the DOM parent is `body`. This surprises people: a click in the modal can trigger a parent row's handler, so stop propagation where needed.

```tsx
import { createPortal } from "react-dom";

function Modal({ open, onClose, children }: ModalProps) {
  if (!open) return null;
  return createPortal(
    <div className="backdrop" onClick={onClose}>
      <div role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="modal-title"
           onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}>
        {children}
      </div>
    </div>,
    document.body,
  );
}
```

**What the portal does not do for you:**
- Focus trap and returning focus to the trigger on close.
- Escape to close, scroll locking, `inert` on the background.
- SSR: `document` does not exist on the server, so render portals only after mount in a client component.

**Modern alternative:** the native `<dialog>` element with `showModal()` renders in the browser's top layer (no z-index fights), traps focus and handles Escape. The Popover API (`popover` attribute) does similar for menus and tooltips. Libraries like Radix use portals plus all the accessibility wiring.

**Lift state to the closest common parent of the components that need it, no higher. To avoid drilling props through layers that do not use them, use composition: let the parent that owns the data render the deep child directly and pass it down as `children` or a slot prop. Reach for context only when many distant components need the same value.**

**Prop drilling example and the composition fix:**

```tsx
// Drilling: Layout and Sidebar only forward `user`
<Layout user={user} />  // -> <Sidebar user={user} /> -> <Avatar user={user} />

// Composition: the owner renders Avatar itself
function Page({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return (
    <Layout sidebar={<Sidebar footer={<Avatar user={user} />} />}>
      <Feed userId={user.id} />
    </Layout>
  );
}

function Layout({ sidebar, children }: { sidebar: ReactNode; children: ReactNode }) {
  return <div className="grid"><aside>{sidebar}</aside><main>{children}</main></div>;
}
```

`Layout` no longer knows what a user is; it is reusable and easier to test.

**Compound components** take this further for widgets: `<Tabs><Tabs.List /><Tabs.Panel /></Tabs>` share state through a small private context, so consumers compose the layout instead of passing a giant config.

**Performance bonus:** JSX passed as `children` is created by the parent above, so when a wrapper's own state changes (e.g. a scroll-tracking container), React sees the same `children` reference and skips re-rendering them.

**Decision order:**
1. Keep state local.
2. Lift to the nearest common parent.
3. Compose with children/slots to skip intermediate layers.
4. Context for truly cross-cutting values (theme, auth, locale).
5. External store for frequently-changing shared state.

**Trade-off:** slots make the owner component bigger; deep composition can be harder to follow than one explicit prop. Pick what keeps each component's responsibility clear.

**A parent `Tabs` component owns the active value and shares it through context. The parts (`Tabs.List`, `Tabs.Trigger`, `Tabs.Panel`) read that context, so callers can arrange and wrap them freely. Add the ARIA tab pattern and keyboard navigation so it is accessible, and support both a controlled `value` and an uncontrolled `defaultValue`.**

**Core implementation:**

```tsx
type Ctx = { value: string; setValue: (v: string) => void; id: string };
const TabsCtx = createContext<Ctx | null>(null);
const useTabs = () => {
  const c = use(TabsCtx);
  if (!c) throw new Error("Tabs parts must be rendered inside <Tabs>");
  return c;
};

export function Tabs(props: { value?: string; defaultValue: string; onValueChange?: (v: string) => void; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [inner, setInner] = useState(props.defaultValue);
  const controlled = props.value !== undefined;
  const value = controlled ? props.value! : inner;
  const setValue = (v: string) => { if (!controlled) setInner(v); props.onValueChange?.(v); };
  return <TabsCtx value={{ value, setValue, id: useId() }}>{props.children}</TabsCtx>;
}

function Trigger({ value, children }: { value: string; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const t = useTabs();
  const selected = t.value === value;
  return (
    <button role="tab" id={`${t.id}-t-${value}`} aria-selected={selected}
      aria-controls={`${t.id}-p-${value}`} tabIndex={selected ? 0 : -1}
      onClick={() => t.setValue(value)}>{children}</button>
  );
}
Tabs.Trigger = Trigger;
// Tabs.List renders role="tablist"; Tabs.Panel renders role="tabpanel" with aria-labelledby
```

**Design decisions to talk through:**
- **Context, not `cloneElement`.** Cloning only reaches direct children and breaks when someone wraps a trigger in a tooltip. Context works at any depth.
- **Accessibility (WAI-ARIA tabs pattern):** `tablist`, `tab` and `tabpanel` roles, `aria-selected`, `aria-controls` and `aria-labelledby`, and a **roving tabindex**: only the selected tab is in the tab order. Left and Right arrows move focus, Home and End jump. Decide between automatic activation (focus selects) and manual activation (Enter or Space selects).
- **Registration order for keyboard navigation:** query the tablist's `[role="tab"]` elements in DOM order on keydown. That is simpler and more robust than keeping a registry in state.
- **Controlled and uncontrolled:** `defaultValue` for simple use, `value` plus `onValueChange` for URL-synced tabs.
- **Panel mounting:** unmount inactive panels (cheaper, but loses state) or keep them mounted and hidden (keeps form state). Expose a `forceMount` option, or use `<Activity mode="hidden">` in React 19.2.
- **TypeScript:** declare `const Tabs = Object.assign(Root, { List, Trigger, Panel })` to get typed static parts.

**Trade-off:** the composed API is flexible but less discoverable. In a design system you often ship the compound primitives *and* a simple wrapper (`<SimpleTabs items={[...]} />`) for the 80% case. Libraries like Radix Tabs and React Aria already implement all of this, and building your own is usually only justified for learning or unusual behaviour.

**Use a custom hook to share stateful logic: it returns values and functions, adds no extra components, and composes without nesting. Use a render prop when the component must own *where and when* caller-supplied UI appears, as in a virtualised list rendering rows, a combobox rendering options, or an error boundary rendering a fallback.**

**The same logic both ways:**

```tsx
// Render prop: the component calls your function with its state
<WindowSize>{({ width }) => (width < 640 ? <MobileNav /> : <DesktopNav />)}</WindowSize>

// Hook: the component consumes the state directly
function Nav() {
  const { width } = useWindowSize();
  return width < 640 ? <MobileNav /> : <DesktopNav />;
}
```

The hook version is flatter, easier to type and easier to combine (`useWindowSize()` plus `useTheme()` plus `useUser()`). With render props, three pieces of shared logic mean three levels of nested callbacks, the "wrapper hell" hooks were introduced to fix in React 16.8, along with higher-order components.

**Where render props still win:**
- **Virtualised lists and grids:** `renderItem={(item, index) => <Row ... />}`. The list decides which items exist in the DOM and positions them. A hook cannot render rows for you inside the list's scroll container.
- **Components that place UI in slots they control:** `renderOption` in a select or combobox, `renderValue`, table cell renderers.
- **Error boundaries:** `react-error-boundary`'s `fallbackRender={({ error, resetErrorBoundary }) => ...}`. Error boundaries must be class components, so there is no hook for catching render errors.
- **Inversion of control in design systems,** where the caller customises rendering but the component keeps behaviour, accessibility and layout.

**Can a hook return JSX?** Technically yes, but it usually hides a component. If a hook returns markup, make it a component (or return props via prop getters, as Downshift and TanStack Table do).

**Performance note:** an inline render function is a new function every render, which only matters if the receiving component is memoised. The React Compiler handles most of these cases now.

**`useOptimistic(state, updateFn)` returns an optimistic copy of `state` plus a function to apply temporary updates. While an Action (a transition) is pending, the UI renders the optimistic value. When the Action finishes, React discards the optimistic layer and renders the real `state` again. If the server data was updated, the change persists. If the action failed, the temporary change simply disappears, and you are responsible for telling the user why.**

**Example: a like button**

```tsx
"use client";
import { useOptimistic, startTransition } from "react";

export function LikeButton({ liked, count, toggle }: { liked: boolean; count: number; toggle: () => Promise<void> }) {
  const [opt, setOpt] = useOptimistic({ liked, count }, (cur, next: boolean) => ({
    liked: next,
    count: cur.count + (next ? 1 : -1),
  }));

  return (
    <button
      onClick={() =>
        startTransition(async () => {
          setOpt(!opt.liked);           // instant feedback
          try {
            await toggle();             // Server Action; revalidates liked/count
          } catch {
            toast.error("Could not save your like"); // the revert is automatic, the message is not
          }
        })
      }
    >
      {opt.liked ? "Unlike" : "Like"} ({opt.count})
    </button>
  );
}
```

**Lifecycle:**

```text
click -> transition starts -> setOpt(...) -> render with optimistic state
      -> await toggle()
         success: server revalidates props -> transition ends -> render real (new) state
         failure: transition ends -> render real (old) state -> optimistic change vanishes
```

**Details that trip people up:**
- `addOptimistic` must run inside an Action or `startTransition`. Outside one, React warns and the value reverts immediately because there is no pending transition to hold it.
- The reducer receives the **current** state (including earlier optimistic updates), so several quick optimistic adds stack correctly.
- Real state must actually change on success, through revalidation (Server Action plus `updateTag` or `revalidatePath`) or a client cache update. Otherwise the UI reverts even though the save worked.
- Mark pending items (`sending: true`) so users can tell confirmed data from guesses.

**Compared with TanStack Query:** there you write `onMutate` (snapshot plus set cache), `onError` (restore snapshot) and `onSettled` (invalidate) yourself, and the optimistic data lives in a shared cache visible to every component. `useOptimistic` is local to one component tree and tied to an Action's lifetime: simpler, but narrower.

**`use(resource)` reads the value of a promise or a context during render. With a promise it suspends the component until the promise resolves (showing the nearest Suspense fallback), returns the value, and sends a rejection to the nearest error boundary. Unlike other hooks it can be called inside `if` statements and loops, because it does not store state in the component's hook list. Each call simply reads a resource.**

**Why ordinary hooks cannot be conditional:** React stores each hook's state in an ordered list on the fiber and matches calls by position. Skip a `useState` in one render and every later hook reads the wrong slot. `use` keeps no per-call slot: for a promise React tracks status on the promise itself, and for context it reads the nearest provider. Call order therefore does not matter. It still must be called **during render** (in a component or hook), not in event handlers or effects.

**Reading a promise:**

```tsx
// Server Component: start the request, do not await it
export default function Page() {
  const statsPromise = getStats(); // promise created once, on the server
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<StatsSkeleton />}>
      <Stats statsPromise={statsPromise} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}

// Client Component
"use client";
function Stats({ statsPromise }: { statsPromise: Promise<Stats> }) {
  const stats = use(statsPromise);   // suspends until resolved
  return <p>{stats.visitors} visitors</p>;
}
```

**Rules:**
- **The promise must be stable.** Creating it during render in a Client Component (`use(fetch(...))`) makes a new promise on every retry, so the component suspends forever. Create it in a Server Component, a loader, an event handler that stores it in state, or a cache.
- **Errors go to error boundaries.** `use` cannot be wrapped in `try/catch`. To provide a fallback value, add `.catch(() => fallback)` to the promise before passing it.
- Promises passed from Server to Client Components must resolve to serialisable values.

**Reading context:**

```tsx
function Toolbar({ readonly }: { readonly: boolean }) {
  if (readonly) return null;
  const theme = use(ThemeContext); // allowed after an early return; useContext is not
  return <div className={theme.toolbar}>...</div>;
}
```

`use(Context)` behaves like `useContext` (same provider lookup, same re-render on change) but can be called conditionally. React recommends it as the more flexible option.

**Interview angle:** `use` is how React 19 makes "data as a promise passed down the tree" a first-class pattern, which is what lets Server Components start fetches early and stream results into interactive client UI.

**Three things changed. Function components now receive `ref` as a normal prop, so `forwardRef` is no longer needed and is slated for deprecation. Ref callbacks can return a cleanup function, which React calls when the element detaches. And string refs were removed for good. Together these make refs simpler and safer to manage.**

**1. ref is a prop**

```tsx
// React 18
const Input = forwardRef<HTMLInputElement, Props>(function Input(props, ref) {
  return <input ref={ref} {...props} />;
});

// React 19
function Input({ ref, ...props }: Props & { ref?: React.Ref<HTMLInputElement> }) {
  return <input ref={ref} {...props} />;
}
```

A codemod migrates existing `forwardRef` usage. Class components still do not receive `ref` as a prop, because there it continues to refer to the class instance. `useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({ focus, scrollToTop }))` works with the prop the same way. Use it to expose a narrow API instead of the raw DOM node.

**2. Cleanup functions from ref callbacks**

```tsx
<div
  ref={(node) => {
    const observer = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => setWidth(entry.contentRect.width));
    observer.observe(node!);
    return () => observer.disconnect(); // runs on detach
  }}
/>
```

Previously React called the callback again with `null` on unmount, and you had to keep the observer somewhere to clean it up. Now setup and teardown sit together, like an effect. A side effect of this: TypeScript rejects implicit returns such as `ref={(el) => (this.el = el)}`, because anything returned is treated as a cleanup. Use a block body instead. A codemod handles this too.

**3. Removed and related changes**
- **String refs** (`ref="input"`), deprecated for years, are removed.
- `element.ref` access is deprecated in favour of `element.props.ref`.
- Related React 19 cleanup: `<Context>` can be rendered directly as a provider, and `propTypes` and `defaultProps` on function components are gone.

**When you still need refs:** focusing and selecting, measuring layout, integrating non-React widgets (maps, charts, editors), and storing mutable values that should not trigger renders. Prefer declarative props for everything else.
